RUNWAY NOTES
Thursday, March 19, 2026

Hiring ahead of the round, or behind it

There is a moment in every raise when the founder has a term sheet in hand, or nearly, and a hiring plan that assumes the money. The question is whether to start the searches now or wait for the wire. The conventional advice is to wait. The conventional advice is mostly wrong, with one big exception.

The argument for waiting is obvious: rounds fall apart, and a company that has hired three people against money that did not arrive has a short, unpleasant year. The argument for starting is that senior searches take four to six months, the round takes two, and the people you want will not be more available in June than they are in March.

The resolution is to separate starting a search from making an offer. Open the searches, run the process, and calibrate the market. Do not extend offers that the current balance sheet cannot cover for twelve months. If the round closes on schedule, you are two months ahead. If it does not, you have a shortlist and no obligations.

The big exception is any hire that changes the story you are telling the investors. If the deck says "we will hire a VP of Sales after the round," and you hire one during diligence, you have introduced a variable into a process that rewards stability. Investors are underwriting a plan; changing it mid-underwriting, even for the better, costs you.

A second, quieter exception: engineering hires that were already open. Pausing an open search because a round is in process signals something to the candidates in the pipeline, and they will draw the wrong conclusion. Keep those running at their normal pace.

Benchmarks: in our dataset, companies that closed a Series A grew headcount by a median 55% in the following twelve months; the top quartile by revenue growth grew headcount by only 40%. The correlation between hiring speed and outcome is negative at this stage, which is worth sitting with.

THIS WEEK'S ROUND-UP
- Q1 venture funding is tracking flat year on year; deal count is down 15%. Bigger checks, fewer companies.

